NOTICE — Weebill
====================

Methodology
-----------

This package is an independent implementation of the Codec 2 3200 bit/s
speech codec, produced by a documented clean-room process.

The implementation was written **from a written specification only**. The
specification (`bitstream.md`, `algorithm.md`, `conformance.md`, the
codebook CSVs and the test vectors) was prepared by a separate "spec side"
of the project, which had access to the reference implementation. The
"clean side" that wrote this code did not read, search, or otherwise
consult the reference implementation's source code, nor any port or
translation of it in any language.

Where the specification was found to be ambiguous, incomplete, or wrong,
the clean side raised a question rather than guessing at what a reference
implementation "probably does". Questions were answered only as revisions
to the specification documents, never as excerpts of reference code. The
full question-and-answer history, including the measurements behind each
resolution, is recorded in the specification's `qa-log.md`.

Conformance is defined as interoperability at equivalent quality, not
bit-exactness. Some frames are provably outside any implementation's
reproducible envelope: on inputs whose autocorrelation is rank-deficient
relative to the 10th-order LPC model (pure tones and sustained single
resonances), the upper line spectral pairs and the synthesis filter gain
are determined by floating-point rounding rather than by the algorithm.
The reference implementation disagrees with its own published bitstream on
such frames when merely recompiled at a different optimisation level. See
`algorithm.md` §6 step 2 and §9.

Authorship
----------

The code in this package was written by an AI system (Claude, by
Anthropic) acting as the clean-side implementer, under human direction and
review, following the process described above. This is disclosed because
it is material to how the clean-room separation should be assessed: the
separation rests on the implementer's inputs having been restricted to the
specification documents listed above, and on the prohibition — stated in
the implementer brief and repeated at each milestone — against reproducing
any recollection of Codec 2's source code from training data. Readers
evaluating the provenance of this work should weigh that prohibition
accordingly and refer to the project's own provenance records.

Third-party interface data
--------------------------

The numeric tables compiled into `Sources/Weebill/Codebooks.swift`
are functional data, not creative expression:

  * `dlspCodebooks` — the ten 32-entry LSP-difference scalar quantiser
    tables (`bitstream.md` §4.3). These are **bitstream interface data**:
    they define what the transmitted indices mean to any decoder, and an
    implementation with different values would not interoperate. They are
    also purely formulaic — arithmetic ladders fully specified by two
    one-line rules (tables 1–3 and 7–10: entry i = 25·(i+1) Hz; tables
    4–6: 25·(i+1) Hz for i = 0..7, then 200 + 50·(i−7) Hz) — so they can
    be generated rather than copied.
  * `nlpFIR48` — the 48-tap 600 Hz low-pass FIR used by the NLP pitch
    estimator (`algorithm.md` §3 step 3). This is **encoder-side
    behavioural data, not bitstream interface**: the decoder never uses it
    and any 64-bit frame is valid regardless. It is pinned so this
    encoder's pitch tracks match the reference encoder, and it is the
    output of a standard public design procedure (a Hamming-windowed sinc
    low-pass, 48 taps, 600 Hz cutoff at 8 kHz, unity DC gain — e.g.
    Octave `fir1(47, 600/4000)`), reproduced by that recipe to within
    3·10⁻⁹ per coefficient.

Both value sets have been verified identical to those used by codec2
v1.2.0, which is what interoperability rests on. They were supplied to the
clean side as CSV data files in the specification package, and are
generated into Swift source by `Scripts/generate-codebooks.sh`. Their
provenance and the generation rules are documented on the specification
side (`provenance/tables-rationale.md`).

